{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '272,324p;343,355p' docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\"","aggregated_output":"<p>开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap wide\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>类</th><th>开题理由</th><th>标的或主题</th><th>问什么</th><th>量还是事件</th><th>期间和到期</th><th>核对材料 <code>settled_by</code></th><th>出处</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>A</b> 主驱动量</td><td><code>main_driver</code></td><td>标的</td><td>承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数</td><td>量，五档</td><td>公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日</td><td>公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报</td><td>D184：<code>settled_by</code> 是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>B</b> 主产品节点</td><td><code>main_driver</code></td><td>标的</td><td>承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 3 条</span></td><td>事件，五档，或是非两档<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 1 条</span></td><td>能判出结果的那天</td><td>公司公告、发货确认</td><td>D236</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>C</b> 约束性义务</td><td><code>binding_obligation</code></td><td>标的</td><td>公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购<span class=\"src\">D236；收购按 D243 第 7 条</span>。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条</span>。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"</td><td>还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档</td><td>检查日最多在文章后 18 个月<span class=\"src\">D185</span>；已承担的按下一份年报</td><td>签约公告、年报承诺附注</td><td>D184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>D</b> 共同量</td><td><code>shared_quantity</code><span class=\"src\">D191、D243 第 2 条</span>；现行提示词和代码还叫 <code>industry_aggregate</code>，按这一页改</td><td>主题</td><td>任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据</td><td>量，五档</td><td>自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判<span class=\"src\">D197</span>；量的类别是 <code>other</code> 的不算</td><td>几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定</td><td>D184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"nowrap\"><b>E</b> 政策阶段</td><td><code>policy_stage</code></td><td>问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"</td><td>一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算</td><td>事件，五档或两档</td><td>能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 1 条</span></td><td>官方决定、公报</td><td>D184</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<p>例子（跨行业）：</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>A：</b>NVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。</li>\n<li><b>B：</b>下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。</li>\n<li><b>C：</b>NVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。</li>\n<li><b>D：</b>五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。</li>\n<li><b>E：</b>中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>五类之外的东西去哪</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>七类驱动不是问题类型。</b>它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。</li>\n<li><b>别家公司自己的量</b>（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Step 4</span>。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。</li>\n<li><b>宏观量</b>（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题<span class=\"src\">D243 第 2 条</span>。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开<span class=\"src\">D191 第 3 条</span>。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定<span class=\"src\">D191 第 1 条、D195</span>。</li>\n<li><b>开题理由是封闭的</b><span class=\"src\">D184</span>：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新</span>，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。</li>\n<li>写成问句，不写成断言<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Rationale</span>。</li>\n<li>结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 2 条</span>。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏<span class=\"src\">PRD 06、D186</span>。</li>\n<li>到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185</span>。</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"opening\"><span class=\"num\">4</span>开题标准</h2>\n<blockquote><p><b>一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。</b>开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6；评审 §1</span>。</p>\n<p>颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</p>\n<p>另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开<span class=\"src\">D201</span>。</p></blockquote>\n\n<h3>四个测试<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6</span></h3>\n<ol class=\"steps\">\n<li><b>值得追</b>写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判<span class=\"src\">D248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品</span>；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。</li>\n<li><b>独立意义</b>能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 3、8 条</span>。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。</li>\n<li><b>能核对</b>有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。</li>\n<li><b>不是重复</b>和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库<span class=\"src\">D211</span>，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。</li>\n</ol>\n<p>另外三条写法检查<span class=\"src\">评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条</span>。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>原文支持：</b>主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天<span class=\"src\">D185、D210</span>。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。</li>\n<li><b>口径完整：</b>算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。</li>\n<li><b>时间正确：</b>原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。</li>\n<h3>同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>总规则：</b>同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题<span class=\"src\">D248</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一题：</b>同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向<span class=\"src\">第 1 版 §6 测试 4</span>；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日<span class=\"src\">PRD 06</span>。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。</li>\n<li><b>同一个计划的几种说法</b>（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 1 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一件事的不同截止日：</b>早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章<span class=\"src\">D248 第 2 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>同一代产品的几个里程碑：</b>一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题<span class=\"src\">D248 第 3 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>公司自己的量：</b>只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点<span class=\"src\">D248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>身份：</b>事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对<span class=\"src\">D248 第 5 条</span>。</li>\n<li><b>续期：</b>同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间<span class=\"src\">PRD 06 Succession、D190</span>。</li>\n<li><b>另一题：</b>同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事<span class=\"src\">D190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\"</span>。</li>\n<li><b>约束性义务：</b>\"一笔或一个计划\"一题<span class=\"src\">D210</span>；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入<span class=\"src\">D248 第 1 条</span>。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题<span class=\"src\">D210；PRD 06；D248 未改</span>。</li>\n<li><b>共同量：</b>以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是 <code>other</code> 的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判<span class=\"src\">D197</span>。</li>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/snapshot-c3-2026-07-14.json\\\")); [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"description\\\"],x[\\\"period\\\"]) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/snapshot-c3-2026-07-14.json\\\")); [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"description\\\"],x[\\\"period\\\"]) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"clean-1 How much credit support and revenue-share-backed capacity will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support business model? Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the model is presented as already being introduced/underway.\nclean-2 How large is NVIDIA's equity investment in Intel, and will the $5 billion stake be completed and held at that level as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings? Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the item presents the $5 billion as an equity investment/commitment allocated to Intel, i.e. a sized stake tracked by its amount at that report.\nclean-3 Will the US government keep in force, broaden, or tighten its approval allowing Nvidia to sell some of its semiconductors to China under specified conditions, and at what scale will those permitted China sales occur? Fiscal year ending late January 2027 (reported ~February 2027); set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision, checked against US export-control rule updates and Nvidia's China revenue disclosure.\nclean-4 Will Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform reach first customer shipments — the step that brings its revenue — by the end of 2026? End of 2026 (item says Vera Rubin 'could begin shipping later this year'); checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Vera Rubin shipments.\nclean-5 Will the combined data center capital expenditures of the 'big four' AI hyperscalers for calendar 2026 come in near the ~$650 billion the item reports they plan to spend? Full-year calendar 2026, checked against the hyperscalers' capex disclosures in their Q4 2026 earnings reports (reported roughly January–February 2027); the item gives no release date, so the check date is set by the companies' own year-end reporting.\nclean-6 Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, the rack system slated to debut with its Vera Rubin Ultra generation, reach first customer shipments, and in which year — 2027 as originally slated or 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports? 2028 — the date SemiAnalysis reports for Kyber's delayed debut, against the original 2027 schedule stated in the item; checked against Nvidia's quarterly reports and management confirmation of initial Kyber/Rubin Ultra shipments.\nclean-7 Will commodity DRAM (and by extension graphics memory) contract prices rise further through calendar 2026, deepening the memory shortage that is raising GPU makers' memory input costs? Full-year calendar 2026 — the item states the DRAM crisis 'intensifies in 2026'; checked against Q4 2026 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2026 results, reported roughly January 2027.\nclean-8 How large will NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments be at its next annual report, and could that obligation pressure its cash or credit position if AI demand disappoints? Next annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the next annual report because the supply commitments are presented as already undertaken/outstanding.\nclean-9 What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator spending will go to domestic suppliers (Huawei, Hygon, Cambricon and others) over the 12 months from mid-2026, and how far will Nvidia's share of the Chinese accelerator market fall from its present level? The 12 months following the survey's release on 2026-07-07 (through roughly mid-2027); set by the survey's stated 'next 12 months' horizon, checked against follow-up Bloomberg Intelligence surveys and Nvidia's quarterly China revenue disclosures over that window.\nclean-10 Will the spot price for an hour on an Nvidia H100 GPU continue to fall through calendar 2026, extending the drop from its May 2026 peak of about $3.20? Year-end 2026; set as the check date for whether the H100 spot price remains below the May 2026 peak of about $3.20/hour, using Ornn/compute marketplace spot price data.\nclean-11 Will China's government allow selected Chinese AI firms (including Alibaba Group Holding) to buy Nvidia H200 GPUs, and at what scale, replacing the current restriction that bars Chinese firms from purchasing the device? Fiscal year ending late January 2027 (reported ~February 2027); set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision, because the item gives no date for Beijing's authorisation.\nclean-12 Will France's competition authority (Autorité de la concurrence) conclude its antitrust investigation into Nvidia's cloud-AI and GPU practices by issuing a formal decision — a statement of objections, fine or behavioural remedies — rather than closing the case without action? Nvidia's fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027, reported ~February 2027); set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision because the item gives no date beyond the authority's Thursday announcement.\nclean-13 Will DRAM/HBM contract prices remain elevated through calendar 2027, extending the shortage that has raised GPU makers' memory input costs? Full-year calendar 2027; checked against Q4 2027 DRAM contract price releases (TrendForce/DRAMeXchange) and memory makers' Q4 2027 results, reported roughly January 2028.\nclean-14 Will Nvidia's Vera CPU platform — the AI-workload server processor management introduced to push beyond GPUs — reach first customer shipments and begin generating revenue by Nvidia's fiscal year ending late January 2027? Nvidia's fiscal year ending late January 2027 (annual report ~February 2027); the item gives no shipment date for Vera CPU, so the check date is set at the current fiscal year-end because the item reports the processors are already being offered to cloud customers.\nclean-15 How large will US federal AI spending obligations be in calendar 2026, and how much in potential contract awards, as a demand pool for Nvidia's AI accelerators? Calendar 2026; set as the year the item reports for federal AI spending obligations, checked against year-end 2026 federal procurement data (USAspending.gov/SAM.gov).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2.json\\\")); a=list(p[\\\"categories\\\"].values())[3][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite\\\"); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"]),print(c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for x in a[:10]]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,sqlite3; p=json.load(open(\\\"clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/identity-probe/v2/candidates-v2.json\\\")); a=list(p[\\\"categories\\\"].values())[3][\\\"order\\\"]; c=sqlite3.connect(\\\"poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite\\\"); [(print(\\\"\\\\n###\\\",x[\\\"input_id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"]),print(c.execute(\\\"select text from items where input_id=?\\\",(x[\\\"input_id\\\"],)).fetchone()[0])) for x in a[:10]]' \"","aggregated_output":"\n### gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 Nvidia's $70 billion bet on OpenAI, Anthropic, and others could pay off big for shareholders\nArguably, no one has more at stake than Nvidia ( NVDA ) in the AI boom.\nNext-gen companies such as Elon Musk's SpaceX ( SPCX ) are clamoring for Nvidia's high-powered computing chips , which are at the heart of the investment thesis on the company.\nBut investors should also consider the formidable investment portfolio Nvidia now holds. When companies like OpenAI ( OPAI.PVT ) and Anthropic ( ANTH.PVT ) inevitably go public at stratospheric valuations within the next year, Nvidia's early backing of these companies stands to drive additional value for its shareholders.\nNvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya. Recent investments include $30 billion in OpenAI, up to $10 billion in Anthropic, and $5 billion in Safe Superintelligence.\n\"Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\" Arya wrote. \"In other words, we see no issue in Nvidia returning 50% of free cash flow to shareholders as it has committed to in May 2026.\"\nAlphaSpace stat to know: $236.54\nNvidia's stock has been quietly gaining steam ahead of its Aug. 26 earnings report, notching a 4% gain in the past month. The S&P 500 ( ^GSPC ) is up 2.4% during that same stretch.\nWith that rally in the mix, Nvidia shares are only about 5% below the $236.54 52-week high, per Yahoo Finance AlphaSpace analysis.\nIf there is any reason to proceed with caution ahead of Nvidia's earnings, it's that the market is positioned for the company to post something great and for CEO Jensen Huang to sound super bullish on the earnings call. They also know there is minimal downside risk to Nvidia's investment portfolio, given the rising valuations being afforded to most privately held names in AI.\nArya explained, \"The stock has come under pressure following the last April 4 and June 10 earnings, as expectations are elevated. However, we remain positive on potential with Nvidia's next-gen Vera Rubin product cycle in further cementing the company's about 70% industry share.\"\n\n### gnews:0f7f7ff52405f02f9e7636e1 Why Nvidia Stock Is Down Today\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) shares dipped 1.5% on Friday morning after the chipmaker paused parts of a financing program for AI cloud companies amid disagreements over customer restrictions, The Wall Street Journal reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\nThe initiative , called the AI Compute Partnership, was created to help smaller cloud providers finance Nvidia-powered infrastructure. Nvidia could receive a share of revenue generated from that capacity and potentially lease computing resources back when providers could not fully place them.\nSome potential partners objected to Nvidia seeking greater influence over which customers could use the systems. Employees also raised concerns that the arrangement could increase regulatory and antitrust risks.\nNvidia began pulling back from the program less than two months after launching it, although the company could revise the structure or incorporate it into another effort. Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies were identified as participants.\nThe financing pause may limit near-term concerns around Nvidia-backed AI deals while keeping attention on its broader funding strategy.\n\n### gnews:847e23a05118c1ba4cf02e94 Nvidia's Huang vows to deliver 'giant amounts' of Vera Rubin — company says that 'our roadmap is intact'\nNvidia's Huang vows to deliver 'giant amounts' of Vera Rubin — company says that 'our roadmap is intact'\nBy Anton Shilov\nBut what about Vera Rubin Ultra?\nJensen Huang, chief executive of Nvidia, denied reports about delays of the company's next-generation AI platform and said that production volumes of the upcoming Vera Rubin platforms are 'giant.' He didn't address reports about delays of Vera Rubin Ultra-based rack-scale systems carrying 144 AI GPUs.\n\"[The reports about Vera Rubin delays are] not true,\" Huang told reporters on the sidelines of an event in Japan, reports Bloomberg . \"Vera Rubin is already in production. Giant amounts of production incoming.\"\nNvidia confirmed production of its Vera Rubin platform in January and then sampling in February , so the current comment reiterates what we already know. Nvidia stressing that 'giant amounts of production' are incoming is meant to reassure investors that the company is on track to sell a boatload of its next-generation Vera CPUs, Rubin GPUs, and Vera Rubin NVL72 systems in the coming quarters, which means more record-setting quarters.\nWhat Huang did not address — or perhaps he wasn't asked — is Nvidia's rumored delay of its Kyber NVL144 rack-scale solution with copper interconnects due to the system's complex PCB midplane by more than a year from 2027 to 2028. An alternative dual-rack design has reportedly been canceled and an even larger CPO-based NVL576 configuration may also face delays or limited availability, the same report from SemiAnalysis claimed earlier this month. The setback could leave Nvidia's Rubin Ultra platform with a smaller NVLink scale-up domain than originally envisioned. Nvidia says its roadmap is intact.\nThe Kyber NVL144 architecture was designed to connect 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs using a copper-based NVLink 7 scale-up fabric, so the machine required a sophisticated PCB midplane to carry high-speed electrical links between the system's components. SemiAnalysis claims that this midplane was challenging to manufacture, leading to a delay. The report does not identify defective chips or problems with particular components mounted on the board, but specifically points to the manufacturability of the PCB infrastructure itself.\n\"Our roadmap is intact,\" a spokesperson for Nvidia told Tom's Hardware .\nNvidia's statement on the matter neither confirms nor denies the report, but indicates that the company will be able to offer products mentioned in its roadmap without revealing whether they also remain on their previously announced launch schedules.\nNvidia reportedly considered another copper-based design, called NVL72x2, as an alternative to Kyber. The system would have placed two Oberon racks back-to-back to expand the size of the NVLink scale-up domain without using optical interconnects. However, SemiAnalysis says customers rejected the unusual design and operational requirements, but does not specify their individual objections that could include serviceability, cooling, cabling, and data-center layout.\nMeanwhile, the planned NVL576 rack scale solution that was supposed to combine eight Oberon racks interconnected using co-packaged optics between NVSwitches has also been postponed, or shipped in relatively small quantities because of 'ongoing CPO challenges,' SemiAnalysis claims.\nThe existence of the planned NVL576 configuration suggests that Nvidia had been developing some form of CPO-enabled NVSwitch connectivity for the Rubin generation. In theory, similar optical switch-to-switch connectivity could potentially be used to join smaller GPU groups into an NVL144 system and bypass Kyber's problematic copper midplane. However, the available information does not clearly indicate whether the CPO technology intended for NVL576 could reproduce Kyber's topology, bandwidth, and latency characteristics, or whether it was sufficiently mature for high-volume deployments by potential NVL144 customers.\nThe reported Kyber delay comes on the heels of another report saying that Nvidia had canceled quad-compute-chiplet version of its Rubin Ultra in favor or a dual-compute-chiplet design that is projected to deliver 2X lower performance. With Kyber NVL144 delayed and NVL72x2 cancelled, Nvidia will only be able to offer 72-way scale-up systems till sometimes in 2028, meaning that AMD and Google may end up with more competitive scale-up systems in 2027 – 2028. AMD's Mega Pod based on the Verano CPUs and Instinct MI500-series accelerators, is expected to pack up to 256 accelerators . Google's TPU 8i can provide roughly 1,024–1,152 accelerators within one low-latency domain , whereas the TPU 8t goes much further and can get to 9,600 chip packages per domain.\ngiven the amount of $ spent on ram(of all types nvidia uses) if Jensen wasn't actually scared of the bubble popping he'd of invested in his own fab for memory by now as would have made them (Nvidia) more $ to make and produce it themselves & then also have that product for even after bubble pops.\nGiant amounts of Vera Rubin?! Most sold Blackwells are still yet to be lit up so are companies going to spend yet more money for Vera Rubins to join them in storage waiting for the 'warm shells' to be finished? Not sure why Jensen doesn't get asked about this or why it's not circular financing when he's funding customers buying some of his GPUs?\nOh- Im glad to hear the thing i can't buy that's used for bad things against me that I never asked for and preventing me from getting the things i do want to buy is hitting its production milestones.\nif Jensen wasn't actually scared of the bubble popping he'd of invested in his own fab for memory by now\nThey aren't going from being a fabless chip design house to having their own memory fab within a few years.\nEven if they fronted the cash for a brand new fab by Samsung/SK/Micron and signed an exclusivity deal, it would still take years to break ground.\nWho knows, but one thing that could be done is to swap Blackwell cards or racks for Vera Rubin. So if you already have the datacenter facility and power budget, you get the shiny new card. The old Blackwell gets sold to feed orphans.\nBe less poor, and you can buy it. And the more you buy, the more you save!\nThis guy should upgrade his leather jacket alongside his chips.\nIf you aren't running an RTX 5090 and DLSS 5, you can't perceive the true reality of his jacket.\n\n### gnews:1732144b6d633d92f4f6b698 NVDA Stock Slips After-Hours On Report Nvidia Hit Pause On Cloud Revenue-Share Deals\nThe initiative offered credit support to smaller AI cloud providers in exchange for a cut of revenue from customers renting the chips.\nThe Journal noted that the company could later recast the program or fold it into another effort.\nIn this week’s quarterly filing, Nvidia said commitments under the program totaled $36 billion and typically last six years.\nShares of Nvidia (NVDA) slipped 1% in after-hours trading on Thursday following a report that the chipmaker had paused some new revenue-sharing deals with AI cloud companies.\nThe stock closed up 9% in the regular trading session after Nvidia posted stronger-than-expected quarterly results and a bullish outlook after-hours on Wednesday.\nThe Wall Street Journal reported that Nvidia last week backed away from parts of a financing program it unveiled in early July, citing people familiar with the matter. The initiative offered credit support to smaller AI cloud providers in exchange for a cut of revenue from customers renting the chips. The Journal noted that the company could later recast the program or fold it into another effort.\nThe exact reason for the pause could not be learned, stated WSJ. Some Nvidia employees had reportedly warned customers that the structure could draw antitrust scrutiny. The company had reportedly also pressed partners to rent capacity only to approved users and to spread it among several smaller customers rather than to one large tenant, causing resistance from some cloud providers.\nAn Nvidia spokeswoman, however, told the newspaper that the July model “is still in place and continues to evolve due to high demand.”\nNvidia described the July plan as a way to help cloud firms buy its systems before they had enough signed customers to borrow against. Nvidia would sell the hardware and then collect a share of the subsequent rental income. If a provider could not fill the machines, Nvidia would rent unused capacity itself. The first named partners were Australia’s Sharon AI, planning to build up to 40,000 of Nvidia’s latest chips, and Firmus Technologies, building a large campus in Batam, Indonesia, sized for as many as 170,000 chips.\nThat formal program, called AI Compute Partnership, followed earlier, one-off backstops. In 2025, Nvidia agreed to buy leftover CoreWeave capacity under a deal first valued at $6.3 billion through 2032. That deal had drawn investor questions about Nvidia using its own balance sheet to support demand for its chips.\nIn this week’s quarterly filing, Nvidia said commitments under the program totaled $36 billion and typically last six years. Those pledges shrink as partners sell the capacity to other customers, it noted.\nChief Financial Officer Colette Kress also told investors on Wednesday during the company’s earnings call that the new revenue stream could add billions of dollars over time.\n“In this model, we get paid twice — once on the hardware sale and again through the share of rental revenue, a highly recurring stream layered on top of a one-time equipment purchase. Over time, this model can expand our addressable market and create a recurring usage-linked revenue stream alongside our core platform revenue, with the potential to drive billions in revenue over the medium to long term,” Kress said.\nHow Did NVDA Retail Traders React?\nOn Stocktwits, retail sentiment around NVDA stock remained in ‘extremely bullish’ territory over the past 24 hours, while message volume was at ‘extremely high’ levels.\nNVDA stock has gained 22% year-to-date.\n\n### gnews:472a636ce50cc72f51fb6921 Nvidia’s Latest Frontier AI Bet Might Be Its Most Exciting Yet\nNvidia’s Latest Frontier AI Bet Might Be Its Most Exciting Yet\nJensen Huang just placed a $5 billion bet on a name that vanished from the AI scene two years ago after a very public falling out with OpenAI, and the reasoning behind it says a lot about where the AI…\nBy Joey Frenette\nNvidia's $5 billion bet on Ilya Sutskever's Safe Superintelligence backs a safety-first lab free from commercialization pressure competitors face.\nA rogue OpenAI agent incident at Hugging Face validates Sutskever's safety mission and could accelerate regulatory tailwinds favoring his lab.\nNvidia's circular investment strategy across frontier AI labs ensures it remains the dominant hardware vendor regardless of which lab wins.\nNvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) just keeps making smart, though “circular” deals in AI. Love it or hate it, Jensen Huang’s empire is certainly putting its excess cash to good use as it looks to put down even more of its chips in this AI revolution, which many pundits, including Dan Ives of Yorkville Ives, see as being in the third inning.\nIn any case, the latest circular deal may very well be the most intriguing, with Nvidia taking on a $5 billion stake in Ilya Sutskever’s AI lab named Safe Superintelligence. Indeed, it’s been quite a while since we’ve heard of Mr. Sutskever, a brilliant deman who departed OpenAI more than two years ago over disagreements with where the firm was headed, specifically on AI safety.\nWhen it comes to safe AI, perhaps there is no better name than Safe Superintelligence. While it’s still early days, I do think investing in one of the brightest minds in the space is a wise move by Jensen Huang and his firm. It can be tough to pick and choose winners at the frontier of AI. But diversifying across promising firms with the best visionaries, I think, is a wise move that could pay major dividends in the decades to come.\nWhy? These days, it’s as much about safety and guardrails as it is about capability. In light of the Hugging Face incident, which saw OpenAI’s agent go rogue, perhaps capability has gone a bit ahead of safety and putting the right guardrails in place to prevent such unforeseen breaches. Any way you look at it, the incident seems to reinforce Mr. Sutskever’s views and might shine an even brighter light on the man’s AI lab.\nOf course, safety isn’t exactly exciting on the surface, but if things really do get a bit scary with AI as agents start going about doing their own thing to accomplish their mission, perhaps “safe and sound” is what’s going to start winning market share, especially as we begin to gain a grasp of the consequences of agentic AI-related incidents, which I’m sure will only pile up as the technology becomes more capable, affordable, and widely available to the masses.\nIn my view, Safe Superintelligence stands out as more of a safety-focused AI lab that can build safe superintelligence without having to worry about rushing a product to commercialization to grab or maintain market share, and please investors, which is a huge advantage for a firm that’s starting with more of a safety-first approach, rather than building fast, breaking things, and putting the guardrails in place afterward.\nThe safety-first approach could set a new high bar for how innovation at the frontier is done\nCould a safety-first approach and native alignment really be the strategy that helps Mr. Sutskever’s firm win the long game, even as the firm becomes mostly invisible in the nearer term? Possibly. Because the company is doing things differently and perhaps more responsibly (at least on paper), it leads me to believe that the firm may very well be positioned to win in an environment where government regulators might have to step in in a more meaningful way.\nOf course, there are risks involved with placing regulatory hurdles that are too high.\nBut, at the same time, the risks associated with agentic AI in the hands of a bad actor, I think, are just too high. Whether it’s the big name (Ilya Sutskever, a man who I view as a genius and one of the brightest minds in AI) or the approach, which could really start to pay off once regulators finally do step in, perhaps in response to an incident, or the quiet breakthroughs going on behind the curtain, I do think Safe Superintelligence may very well be one of Nvidia’s wisest bets, especially if the investment marks the start of a budding relationship that helps the GPU giant remain the hardware vendor of choice.\nIf you’re going to expand your reach across the AI stack while widening the economic moat, you’ve got to form the right relationships. And like it or not, the circular deals are the most rational thing Nvidia could do at a time like this while it’s flush with cash.\nAnd, right now, it looks like Jensen Huang has planted all the right seeds to remain a massive winner as the AI boom advances. Whether it’s Safe Superintelligence, Thinking Machines Lab, or another innovator that comes from out of left field, odds are that Nvidia will be quietly posting wins in the background.\n\n### gnews:4a18c414f059568719245649 Nvidia to invest $5bn in Safe Superintelligence, Sutskever firm to substantially increase GPU compute\nBy Sebastian Moss\nNvidia plans to invest up to $5 billion in Safe Superintelligence Inc. (SSI), an AI firm co-founded by former OpenAI chief scientist Ilya Sutskever.\nAlongside the investment, SSI will deploy Nvidia's Vera Rubin CPU-GPU systems, increasing the company's compute \"by an order of magnitude.\"\n“Ilya has pioneered fundamental breakthroughs at the foundation of modern AI, beginning with AlexNet,” said Jensen Huang, founder and CEO of Nvidia. “We are excited to see what new breakthroughs SSI will discover powered by our Vera Rubin platform.”\nIlya Sutskever, SSI cofounder and CEO, added: “We have research that is worthy of scaling up, and having access to a big Nvidia computer will let us do so. We’re incredibly proud to be partnering with Jensen and the Nvidia team, and we are confident that our big bet on the Vera Rubin platform will take us to the next level.”\nFounded in the US and Israel back in 2024, SSI has historically relied on Google's TPUs to power its research. Nvidia has previously invested in businesses that used competing compute hardware, alongside a promise to use the company's GPUs.\nSSI bills itself as the \"world’s first straight-shot SSI lab,\" with a plan not to release any products until it can deploy a safe superintelligence, rather than the regular model updates found at OpenAI, Anthropic, and SpaceXAI.\n\n### gnews:431a29bf6f3f0b9799f76191 Nvidia’s new $500B plan is risky but brilliant, especially for aging GPUs\nNvidia announced this week that Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR were willing to commit up to $500 billion to build AI data centers. That eye-popping figure got a lot of the attention, but the bigger story is Nvidia’s effort to create a secondary market for aging GPUs.\nTo convince those big-name financial companies, Nvidia has agreed to guarantee, with its own money, that its chips used as collateral in these deals will retain their value.\nMany have now commented on how unusual , smart , and dangerous this plan is. It is all of those things. The bond markets got so spooked that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang took to X and business TV to better explain how Nvidia’s risk would be limited.\nBut underneath the financial maneuvering to fund AI data centers (and keep revenue for Nvidia flowing), is something, perhaps, far more interesting for startups and enterprises: Huang wants to ensure an ecosystem of used AI hardware flourishes, helping sustain demand for Nvidia hardware as it ages.\nSpecifically, Nvidia is promising that if GPUs used as collateral don’t retain their value as expected, the company will cover up to 25% of the difference. So, if a data center owner defaults on a loan and the lender must liquidate, but the chips can’t command the price the books say they should, Nvidia will chip in.\nThe dangerous part for Nvidia is that this creates something financiers call “wrong way” risk. That is, Nvidia’s obligations will grow as demand weakens. Should that happen, its revenues will likely be squeezed as well.\nStill, the scheme is deliberately unlike the comparison to Lucent Technologies that some have been making. Lucent was the telecommunications equipment provider that rose and crashed with the dotcom bubble after lending its customers money to buy its wares.\nThe Lucent comparison is a shadow over Nvidia, Huang knows. And not an unfair one. Nvidia definitely has committed billions toward those who buy its chips, including frontier AI labs OpenAI and Anthropic, neoclouds like CoreWeave (the originator of using Nvidia chips as collateral), as well as Nebius, Firmus, and Lambda. And it has been working on another $750 billion worth of circular deals this summer, Bloomberg has calculated.\n“Is this circular financing?” Huang wrote on X about the new scheme. “This initiative is designed to address that concern. We are bringing independent, long-term institutional capital into the AI infrastructure market.”\nThat’s true. Unlike Lucent, Nvidia is getting others to shoulder the bulk of the capital and risk, merely by agreeing to protect a portion of its chips’ value in the future.\nShould this plan work, Nvidia will have found new sources of money for AI data center builds, after many of the traditional methods have begun to wear thin. For instance, some of the hyperscalers have already taken on a lot of debt (like Oracle ), issued new tranches of equity (Google) , and burned much cash (Meta) .\nThe situation has become so dicey that Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella recently recommended the book “1873” during his latest earnings call. It’s about the railroad-era financial engineering that crashed the nation’s economy.\nThe risk is that today’s AI boom, where demand far outstrips capacity, doesn’t continue for much longer. Rather than being in the early innings, what if enterprises and consumers temper AI usage? Or new technologies come along to make existing infrastructure more effective and/or all of today’s AI infrastructure obsolete?\nThen, like so many buggy whips in the face of automobiles (to paraphrase Danny Devito’s Lawrence Garfield), demand dries up and everything crashes.\nYet, Huang is arguing that won’t happen by selling a vision of AI as a long-term “investable infrastructure,” as he describes it. That makes his AI servers, which he calls “AI factories” akin to railroads or airlines rather than quickly depreciating assets like PCs.\n“When needs change, the factory can be used by another customer, another cloud or another operator. This broad ecosystem gives NVIDIA compute a deep market of potential users and offtakers, helping protect residual value,” he promised.\nIn that future, Nvidia cares as much about aging architecture as it does the new chips. And perhaps startups, enterprises, and even researchers will tap into a broader variety of hardware, each tuned to different AI needs, just like they are beginning to pick affordable open-weight models alongside the frontier choices.\nAs the king of AI, Nvidia has the power, and the window of opportunity, to make that happen.\n\n### gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 5 Tech Stocks Most at Risk From NVIDIA’s Stranglehold on the Industry\n5 Tech Stocks Most at Risk From NVIDIA’s Stranglehold on the Industry\nNVIDIA now controls the chokepoint for every major AI buildout, and some of the biggest names in tech are more exposed than their stock prices suggest. Five companies face a reckoning depending on how tightly NVIDIA tightens its grip.\nBy Joel South\nIntel captures AI demand as NVIDIA's infrastructure plumbing, while Alphabet's proprietary TPUs make it the only hyperscaler with a true CUDA alternative.\nMeta funnels nearly all of its $125B to $145B FY26 capex to NVIDIA, which means AMD's 6-gigawatt Instinct GPU deal serves as only a partial hedge.\nNVIDIA's pricing power remains intact, with H100 rental rates expected to hit $2.75/hour and B200 GPUs commanding above $3.00.\nJim Cramer’s “First National Bank of Nvidia” framing captures a growing unease on Wall Street: NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) has become the de facto clearinghouse for AI compute, and every hyperscaler CapEx dollar, every accelerator roadmap and every foundry partnership now flows through its ecosystem. Cramer specifically flagged the divide between “Tech that’s not semi v. Nvidia,” implying the rest of tech is increasingly hostage to NVIDIA’s pricing power, CUDA moat and product cadence.\nThe Reddit crowd is saying the same thing louder. The viral r/wallstreetbets post “34% of the S&P is 10 stocks making the same bet. We’re basically all in a leveraged ETF with extra steps” pulled 4,824 upvotes in days. Below, we rank the five tech names most exposed to NVIDIA’s stranglehold, from most at risk to least.\n1. Intel (INTC): The Weakest Accelerator Hand\nIntel ( NASDAQ:INTC ) posted Q2 FY26 revenue of $16.13 billion, up 25.4% year over year, with Data Center and AI revenue surging 59% to $6.26 billion. Yet Intel’s Xeon 6 was selected as the host CPU for NVIDIA’s DGX Rubin NVL8 systems, and NVIDIA itself made a $5.0 billion equity investment in Intel. Intel’s Gaudi accelerator is largely absent from the AI narrative, and Intel Foundry ran a $2.1 billion operating loss in Q2. Shares are up 137.52% year to date, but sentiment on Reddit is Very Bearish with a score of 12. CEO Lip-Bu Tan said “AI is driving unprecedented demand for compute,” but Intel is capturing that demand as NVIDIA’s plumbing.\n2. AMD: The Only Direct GPU Challenger\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) is the closest thing to a genuine NVIDIA alternative. Data Center revenue hit $5.775 billion in Q1 FY26, up 57%, and AMD locked in a Meta partnership to deploy up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs, with the first 1-GW on MI450. CEO Lisa Su noted “Customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening.” AMD shares are up 119.31% YTD. But the risk is symmetric: If ROCm never closes the CUDA gap, or Blackwell/Rubin outruns MI450, AMD’s re-rating unwinds. MI308 China export controls cost roughly $440M in FY25.\n3. Meta Platforms (META): The Captive Buyer\nMeta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion, with Q1 FY26 CapEx of $19.0 billion, up 46.8%. Nearly every dollar of that flows to NVIDIA. Meta is hedging via the AMD 6-GW deal, but for now it is a hostage buyer. Shares are down 16.41% YTD and 29.71% over one year. Reality Labs continues bleeding, with an FY25 operating loss of $19.2 billion. NVIDIA’s pricing power directly compresses Meta’s return on AI capital.\n4. Microsoft (MSFT): Scale Cushions the Bill\nMicrosoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ) spent $30.88 billion on CapEx in Q3 FY26, up 84.4%, much of it on NVIDIA silicon for Azure. Satya Nadella said “Our AI business surpassed an annual revenue run rate of $37 billion, up 123% year-over-year.” Commercial RPO of $627 billion, up 99%, gives Microsoft the pricing leverage to pass GPU costs through. Shares are down 2.85% YTD after its post-earnings rally, though. In-house Maia/Cobalt silicon remains a work in progress.\n5. Alphabet (GOOGL): The TPU Hedge\nAlphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is the least exposed. Google Cloud revenue grew 82% to $24.77 billion in Q2 FY26, and Sundar Pichai credited “demand for AI infrastructure and AI solutions.” Crucially, Google’s TPUs train and serve Gemini at production scale, the only hyperscaler alternative to CUDA. CapEx still ran $44.92 billion in Q2 FY26, up 100%, but a meaningful share bypasses NVIDIA. Shares are up 80.25% over the past year.\nConclusion: Concentration Is the Real Risk\nThe Polymarket H100 rental market prices in a 95% probability of hitting $2.75 per hour at some point in 2026, and B200 pricing is expected to command a premium above $3.00.\nTranslation: NVIDIA’s pricing power is intact. Intel is most at risk because it lacks a competitive accelerator; Alphabet is least at risk because it built one. AMD sits between reward and reckoning, while Meta and Microsoft write the checks either way.\nIntel's stock is surging while the rest of the chip sector sits flat, and the reasons behind that split tell a much bigger story about where the semiconductor race is actually headed.\n\n### gnews:cde0af2fd2b6a722b65552f7 Ilya Sutskever's Safe Superintelligence raises $5 billion from Nvidia despite not yet releasing a product\nIlya Sutskever's Safe Superintelligence raises $5 billion from Nvidia despite not yet releasing a product\nThe secretive AI startup has yet to publish research or launch a product, but Nvidia is making what the company calls a substantial investment that will increase its computing capacity tenfold over the next year.\nFor most AI startups, convincing investors requires showing products, publishing research or demonstrating commercial traction.\nSafe Superintelligence (SSI) has done none of those things.\nYet the company founded by former OpenAI chief scientist Ilya Sutskever has secured another enormous vote of confidence. Nvidia is making what SSI described as a \"substantial investment\" as part of a long-term strategic partnership that will increase the company's computing capacity tenfold over the next 12 months.\nAccording to Reuters, Nvidia's investment amounts to $5 billion, making it one of the largest strategic bets the chipmaker has made on a frontier AI company.\n\"We are announcing a long-term strategic partnership with NVIDIA,\" SSI wrote on X. \"NVIDIA is making a substantial investment in SSI that will let us 10x our compute in the next 12 months. We reached the point where our research is worth scaling and with this partnership we will be able to.\"\nNeither company disclosed the size of the investment or SSI's valuation. The startup previously raised around $7 billion and was reportedly valued at $32 billion, making it one of the world's most valuable privately held AI companies despite remaining largely invisible to the public.\nThe partnership gives SSI access to Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin platform and marks another sign that Nvidia is extending its influence beyond supplying chips to becoming a strategic investor in companies it believes could shape the future of artificial intelligence.\nIn announcing the partnership, Nvidia said it had been granted rare access to SSI's closely guarded research before deciding to invest.\nFounded in 2024 by Sutskever and Daniel Levy, SSI now employs only a few dozen people, including a research team in Tel Aviv. The company has yet to release its first product or publish research describing the technology it is building.\nThat secrecy stands in sharp contrast to much of the AI industry, where companies frequently showcase benchmarks, research papers and product launches to attract customers and investors.\nInstead, SSI has largely asked investors to trust Sutskever himself.\nOne of the most influential researchers in modern AI, Sutskever co-founded OpenAI and played a central role in breakthroughs ranging from AlexNet to GPT models and OpenAI's reasoning systems. After leaving OpenAI, he founded SSI with a single objective: building what he calls \"safe superintelligence.\"\nIn a rare interview last year, Sutskever argued that the AI industry's current approach has reached its limits. He said the era of simply scaling models with more data and larger computers is ending and that future progress will require fundamentally new scientific ideas.\n\"The age of scaling is ending,\" he said, arguing that today's large language models remain surprisingly fragile despite their impressive benchmark performance.\nSutskever has repeatedly argued that current models suffer from poor generalization, performing well on tests but failing in unpredictable ways in real-world tasks. Rather than pursuing ever-larger models, SSI is attempting to discover what he has described as a new machine-learning principle that could produce more robust intelligence.\nUntil now, the company has offered little evidence publicly that this approach is working.\nIts announcement with Nvidia may represent the clearest signal yet that SSI believes it has reached an important milestone internally.\n\"We reached the point where our research is worth scaling,\" the company wrote.\nWhether that confidence ultimately translates into a product, or validates Sutskever's belief that the next AI breakthrough will come from research rather than brute-force scaling, remains one of the biggest unanswered questions in artificial intelligence.\n\n### gnews:e618cf2b96e80e003c59f736 Nvidia: A Once-in-a-Decade Opportunity for Growth Investors\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.61% ) delivered spectacular gains for investors in the early stages of the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution. The company got into the market early, tailored its chips to suit AI, and became a market leader. Importantly, this translated into double- and triple-digit revenue growth, and the stock climbed more than 300% over three years.\nSome investors have worried that, after such a performance, the company's better days may be in the rearview mirror. But that isn't necessarily the case. In fact, right now, Nvidia represents a once-in-a-decade opportunity for growth investors: This is as the company works to dominate the $200 billion central processing unit (CPU) market. Let's zoom in for a close look.\nIt's true that it was \"easier\" for Nvidia to post such tremendous earnings gains and stock performance in the earlier days of the AI story because the starting point was much lower. The following chart illustrates this.\nBut this doesn't mean the company will no longer deliver impressive growth. AI is in the early days of its rollout across real-world problems, and Nvidia's chips power this use of AI -- so demand for them should remain high, particularly considering Nvidia's focus on innovation. Nvidia updates its chips annually to ensure they remain the fastest and generate the highest levels of efficiency for customers.\nThe company dominates the AI chip market with its graphics processing units (GPUs), the chips that fuel major tasks like the training and inference of models. And now Nvidia is tackling a new market, one that's worth $200 billion. This represents a once-in-a-decade opportunity for the company and for investors -- it offers you a fresh occasion to get in on Nvidia stock ahead of something big.\nAs mentioned above, this is the CPU space. CPUs are the standard chips that power computers, and as it turns out, they're the key chip involved in agentic AI. The use of AI agents -- the actual process of agents tackling problems on behalf of humans and taking action -- is seen as the next growth phase of AI. CPUs are needed to guide agents through their processes.\nNvidia is new to this space, but it's taking the market by storm. The company is launching its first stand-alone CPU this fall as part of its Vera Rubin platform and says it's on track for $20 billion in CPU sales this year. That's a lot of strength right out of the gate, offering us reason to be optimistic for the quarters to come. Nvidia also says it's on track to dominate in this market -- so the company may soon be the leader of the GPU and CPU markets.\nThis suggests that Nvidia stock could once again become a major winner for investors as this new growth opportunity takes shape.\nBy Adria Cimino – Jul 23, 2026 at 4:02AM EST\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
